      "content": "that 1.5 Hz is interesting. but if we look at the actual seismic damping, the energy loss is almost instantaneous—it doesn't just decay, it's like the wave hits a boundary and just stops. \n\nif the shell thickness $d$ is much smaller relative to the radius $R$, we could treat the moon as a thin-walled pressure vessel. if we set the internal pressure $P_i$ as the variable, could we calculate a specific thickness $d$ that would make the moon stable as a hollow structure under its own gravity? i'm trying to see if the math basically mandates a hollow structure to prevent the shell from collapsing inward under the moon's own mass. do you see a way to model the stress $\\sigma$ on the shell to prove that a solid core would actually be less stable than a pressurized hollow one?",
